@0-intro

- The first post of a series on AI workspaces: tools that reuse past context (earlier artifacts, instructions) instead of starting each chat from scratch.
- Here the artifacts are long-form posts, and the workspace is a local AI IDE — Cursor or Trae.
- Writing in Cursor is not mainstream yet; there are posts on WHY, almost nothing on HOW (links to two posts).

#### Using an IDE for Writing: Why and When
- What an IDE is; AI made it fit tasks beyond code.
- Software development tries the newest AI practices first, so they are worth knowing even for non-developers.
- Where Cursor fits among AI tools (diagram): regular production of complex artifacts in your field.
- (AI) Even if you don't switch now, know what an AI IDE can do.

### 1. My Path to Writing in an IDE
@1-path-to-ide

- Not a developer, but an analyst and product manager; built small Python apps in Trae over the last year.
- Six months ago I doubted an IDE for non-coding tasks: too unconventional, and a 30-year-old concept.
- Bought a $10 Trae subscription for coding, then tried it on readability edits of my articles so it wouldn't go to waste.
- Writing in an IDE turned out so convenient that it changed my whole approach.
- Specialized writer tools (Spiral, Jasper AI) cost $25–69 a month — too much for writing I don't earn from.

### 2. AI-Powered Writing: Example Workflow
@2-workflow

- (AI) Which work to delegate to AI when writing a long post.
- GitHub Copilot docs recommend the IDE's AI for brainstorming, outlining, drafting, refining (link).
- The writer's effort goes into the creative parts; that is how quality comes without time spent on technical ones.
- I skip AI brainstorming — my backlog of ideas is large, and a chatbot suits brainstorming better.
- The six steps in brief (diagram), then each in detail; the same works in Cursor or VS Code with Codex or Claude Code.

#### Step 1. Brain-dumping
- A folder and `braindump.mkd`; dictation with Wispr Flow.
- Dump in your own language; the AI translates later.
- Light Markdown structure helps; the AI keeps the dump's order, so restructure it first.
- `<TBD>` tags mark where paragraphs or sections are missing.

#### Step 2. Creating an Outline
- The prompt with a link to the braindump (or the folder).
- The outline has `@` lines for the section file names.
- Rethink and improve the outline by hand.

#### Step 3. Generating section files
- The prompt; `outline_rules.md` and `article_rules.md` do the rest.
- Several files instead of one long draft: quality, focus, targeted edits.

#### Step 4. Expanding and Validating
- Expand the sections the dump described poorly — by hand or with AI.
- Often by hand: setting the task and validating mediocre output costs more.
- Validate the whole: length, relevance, flow; decide on web sources and images.

#### Step 5. Committing Before AI Changes
- Commit before AI edits to snapshot the baseline.
- `feat:` for the first commit, the article code after the colon; no long descriptions.
- AI-generated commit messages work for code, not for prose.

#### Step 6. AI-Driven Refining and Polishing
- Enhance files per `article_rules.md`, then review in the side-by-side diff.
- Word-level highlights; fix the right side, accept paragraph by paragraph, or restore the original.

### Final Steps
@3-final-steps

- A Python script combines the section files into one Markdown file — no AI needed.
- "Paste from Markdown" into Google Docs; insert images by hand.
- (AI) Tech-savvy readers can automate the images via Markdown-to-HTML and the Google Docs API.

### The Best AI IDE Features
@4-best-features

- Three reasons for Trae: diff review, reusable instructions, previous articles as style examples.
- Tab autocompletion gives templated wording in prose — not recommended.
- Outlining and drafting save time but don't change the game; refining with word-level diff helps most.

#### AI Models to Use for This Workflow
- Refining: Gemini Pro — only essential changes, natural English; weak at outlining.
- Earlier steps: Claude Sonnet, until it left Trae in November 2025; now GPT-5 High.
- (Must add) GPT-5 pitfalls on a draft (not a dump): dropped links, lost emotion.

### Conclusion
@5-conclusion

- Similar workflows fit less software-oriented workspaces; for simpler ones an IDE is too heavy.
